{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. We have some fun \nnew merch at the merch store,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3560,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll let you know more at the end of the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Warp drives may or may not be possible, \nbut if they are then could a distant alien  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12760,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilization’s warp fields produce \ngravitational waves that we could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17160,
    "dDurationMs": 11160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see here on Earth? Well according to \na recent study that may be the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are entering a golden era of gravitational \nwave astronomy. Over the past nine years LIGO - "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34720,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the laser interferometer \ngravitational wave observatory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37480,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has found around 90 mergers between black \nholes or neutron stars, with sensitivity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing with each new observing run. Using \neverything we’ve learned from LIGO, we’re now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planning the next generation of detectors, \nlike Cosmic Explorer, the Einstein telescope,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the newly approved Laser Interferometer Space \nAntenna LISA. We have a good idea of some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60040,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what these detectors will see—from spinning \nwhite dwarfs to merging supermassive black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes. But what will they find that we aren’t \nexpecting? Whenever we open a new window to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70480,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe we find unexpected phenomena. \nMaybe in one of these windows there be aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78760,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Specifically, if there are advanced \nalien civilizations out there,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82520,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if these civilizations travel the \nuniverse with warp technology, could we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87680,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect signatures of the massive distortion in \nspacetime caused by those warp bubbles? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95600,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we wouldn’t be doing this episode if the answer \nwasn’t at least a remote maybe. Specifically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102600,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new study finds that the collapse of \na warp bubble of moderate size within  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our galactic neighbourhood should be visible \nas gravitational waves for future detectors.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113760,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s pick this apart to see if and when \nwe’ll discover our first alien warp bubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First a recap. Warp drives have long been a \nuseful tool in science fiction to evade the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124280,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pesky speed limit of the universe: lightspeed. \nAnd then, back in 1994, Mexican physicist Miguel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132360,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alcubierre found a solution for the superluminal \nwarp field within the classical framework of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity. Obviously we covered \nthis in a previous video.The loophole that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alcubierre used is that, while it’s impossible \nto travel through space faster than light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150800,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s no limit to the speed with which a patch \nof space can move relative to another patch. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know for sure that this superluminal relative \nmotion of patches of space occurs. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162680,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two sufficiently distant regions of the universe \nare moving away from each other faster than light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168000,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to the expansion of the universe. And we can \nthink of the space beneath the event horizon of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172920,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a black hole as moving at superluminal \nspeeds relative to the distant exterior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178560,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Alcubierre warp field works by \nexpanding space behind a patch of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184040,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and contracting it in front, which ends \nup pushing this bubble of space forward. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189760,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because general relativity allows it, there’s \nno fundamental limit to the speed that bubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can reach. Now just put a spaceship in \nthe bubble and you have a warp ship."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200040,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why don’t we have our own warp drives \nyet? Well, because they may be impossible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206720,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even if they’re possible they \nhave some pretty insane requirements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211800,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Alcubierre warp field is a valid solution \nto the Einstein equations. Those equations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relate the geometry of space with the mass \nand energy that space contains. The geometry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is captured by the metric on the left side of \nthe Einstein equations. Almost any imaginable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228880,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime geometry can be described by the \nmetric, including the Alcubierre warp field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235800,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The equations of general relativity then tell you \nwhat distribution of mass and energy space needs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243040,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to have in order to be warped into your chosen \nmetric—that’s the stress-energy tensor on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249240,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right. But not every imaginable metric corresponds \nto a physically possible mass-energy distribution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257240,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Alcubierre metric looks to be one such \nimpossible configuration. For one thing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262320,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it violates something called the \nnull energy condition. Put simply,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to curve space as the warp metric demands, \nthe corresponding stress-energy tensor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demands a space containing an enormous \namount of negative mass. Unfortunately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this so-called exotic matter is almost certainly \nnot possible. Workarounds have been discussed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like using the Casimir effect. Erik Lentz even \ncame up with a warp solution that may not require  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287960,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative mass. Although, even this suffers the \nother big problem with the Alcubierre field—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294000,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of energy required to move even a \nsmall vessel capable of carrying people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is estimated to be the mass-energy equivalent of \nanything between a large moon to an entire star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305680,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have videos on these recent alternative \nproposals, but the new paper by Katy Clough,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311240,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tim Dietrich and Sebastian Khan goes back to the \nOG Alcubierre field. They make no claims about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318600,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical or practical possibility of constructing \nthis field in the first place. Instead, they ask  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what really happens to a warp bubble after \nthe fact, assuming it somehow gets made. And,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329760,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of utmost importance, does a warp bubble produce \ngravitational waves that we could detect?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the Alcubierre metric describes a \nbubble of space that can travel faster  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342320,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than light. When in motion at a constant velocity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the warp bubble should not produce \nany gravitational waves. It should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350200,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce them when accelerating or slowing down or \nperhaps when forming or switching off. In short,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356560,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the warp field is undergoing some evolution \nover time beyond constant motion. Unfortunately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Alcubierre solution doesn’t say anything \nabout how the metric should evolve over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is what the researchers explore in \ndetail in their paper—the natural evolution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373640,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of an Alcubierre bubble according to general \nrelativity. This is no easy calculation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380960,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Understanding the time evolution of a metric \nand its corresponding mass-energy distribution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requires something called an equation of \nstate. In the paper, the authors point out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392080,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that there is no known equation of state that \nwould sustain the required metric over time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means it’s not enough to just have exotic \nmatter, you also need a yet-unknown behavior of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404520,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that matter to lead to the also-yet-unknown \nequation of state to sustain the bubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s assume some advanced civilization \nfigures out how to make exotic matter and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416080,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contain it so that it holds together \nfor the journey across the galaxy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, at constant velocity spacetime would be \nperfectly flat far away, meaning there wouldn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426560,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be any observable gravitational waves. But \nwe also know that the warp bubble does not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hold its shape if the delicately engineered drive \nstops constraining exotic matter with the required  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437480,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation of state. So what happens when the drive \nis switched off? Or what happens if it fails?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the latter case it’s probably very bad for \nthe aliens, but may be very good for us because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449680,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it could produce observable gravitational \nwaves. So how do we calculate what happens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you burst a warp bubble? It’s not easy.\nWhile Einstein’s equations are very elegant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculations can quickly become horrendously \nmessy. After all, we’re trying to evolve complex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distributions of matter through 4-D spacetime. \nFor this reason the researchers used numerical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472920,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity simulations—which means calculating \neach time step based on the previous time step."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480960,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To do this they needed to split 4-D \nspacetime into slices of 3-D space—or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487400,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypersurfaces—at consecutive times. The \naim is a sort of flip-book in which each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493120,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "page is a 3-D spatial volume, and flicking \nthrough the pages gets us to the evolution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498680,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that space and so reconstructs a 4-D \nspacetime containing an evolving metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505280,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So they rewrite the Alcubierre metric in terms \nof a three dimensional metric that lives on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypersurface and introduce a few functions that \ntell us how to translate between different ways  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of slicing—different hypersurfaces—and \nhow they embed into the overarching 4D  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522520,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime. The starting metric on the first \npage of the flipbook has a corresponding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528080,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initial configuration of matter, and that \nmatter should evolve as the metric evolves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far so good. Up until now, this has all been \npretty standard practice for simulating general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539640,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity. This is the same framework we’d use \nto tackle more realistic, dare I say mundane  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545840,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problems, like the merger of black holes. But \nsimulating warp drives is not such smooth sailing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that we need this equation-of-state thing \nin order to evolve the Alcubierre metric and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corresponding mass-energy distribution over \ntime. And remember also that we don’t know of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an equation of state that allows this metric to be \nstable. But that’s OK, because we’re trying to see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569200,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what happens when the warp bubble fails. This \nmeans its possible to come up with a workable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573960,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation of state for a failing warp bubble with, \nadmittedly, a few speculative assumptions about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things like the pressure, timescales of the decay, \nand the resulting matter after the collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586640,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if these assumptions about the \nequation of state of non-existent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter are about as accurate as assuming a \nspherical Klingon in a frictionless vacuum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the point is that even with sensibly imperfect \nassumptions we can still learn something about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the physics. As well as progress with \nthe calculation, which is always nice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606320,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, we’ve set up the first page of our \nflipbook—one of our hypersurfaces with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a matter configuration, which in turn gives us \nour desired Alcubierre metric. We’ve specified  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617880,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the equation of state, and so we’re ready to \nsimulate. By specifying these initial data,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625880,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s equations and the equation \nof state take over and the computer can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630080,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evolve the geometry of spacetime and \nthe distribution of matter from one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypersurface to the next. Once we reach \nthe final hypersurface flipbook page  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639440,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can stitch together spacetime again and \nwatch as it evolves through the simulation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646520,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, how exactly does it evolve?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say an advanced alien species takes a \ntrip to see their cosmic neighbours. On this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "occasion they allow the new recruit to pilot \nthe warp drive. Everything’s going great until  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660240,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they miss a warning light coming from the matter \ncontainment field and… the warp bubble bursts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667000,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "destroying the ship and crew in the \nprocess. We’ve all been there. Or,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670800,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depending on exactly how this stuff works, \nmaybe this was a controlled dispersion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the warp bubble once they reached \nthe destination. But the catastrophic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678680,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "version is likely to make bigger waves, and also \nbetter represents the simulation in this paper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first thing that happens is that the \nwarp bubble collapses inwards, presumably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689600,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obliterating the ship, and then expands \noutwards, all at the speed of light. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693480,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outward expansion is accompanied by a \nseries of gravitational waves—ripples  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that follow the massive fluctuations in \nspacetime curvature of the ruptured bubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what would these waves look like \nto us? The signal shape would be very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710880,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different to the black hole or neutron \nstar mergers we’ve seen. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715640,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the researchers likened the signal to the \nhead-on collision between black holes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but without the characteristic ringdown at the \ntail end as the merged black hole settles down.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725520,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means, if we could detect an event, it would \nstand out as not being from a natural source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But could we detect these gravitational waves \nin the first place? The study calculates that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737840,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the collapse of a 1km radius warp bubble traveling \nat 10% light speed would produce a signal at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745280,
    "dDurationMs": 7632,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth with a strain of 10^-21 if it happened one \nmegaparsec away - that’s around 3 million light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752912,
    "dDurationMs": 8048,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years - or a bit further away that the Andromeda \ngalaxy. A strain of 10^-21 means lengths here  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760960,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be shifted back and forth by a factor \nof one over a billion trillion. Sounds tiny,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that’s exactly the strain limit that LIGO \ncan detect. So can LIGO detect bursting warp  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774840,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubbles in Andromeda? Not quite. The frequency \nof this 10%-lightspeed kilometer-radius warp  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782440,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble’s gravitational waves are 300kHz, which \nis way higher than LIGO’s sensitivity range."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790680,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While high frequency detectors operating in the \nMega to Giga hertz range are being considered,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797360,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "focus for now is on much lower \nfrequency sources—the larger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slower natural phenomena that are much more \nlikely to be real. So, for the time being,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808520,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t expect headlines saying we’ve \ndetected the gravitational wave signature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a stalled hyper-advanced \nextraterrestrial warp drive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816920,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s pretty exciting that the detector capable \nof seeing such a thing is within our technological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824320,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grasp. And although any signal is well outside \nLIGO’s frequency, it’s possible that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830920,
    "dDurationMs": 9000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational waves from a burst warp bubble will \nappear even stronger than the 10^-21 strain. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839920,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, if it’s from within our own galaxy then \nit’s much closer than 1 megaparsec and so will be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847120,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“louder”. If the warp bubble is traveling faster \nthan 0.1c then it emits more intense gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854360,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves on bursting. But 10% lightspeed requires a \nmass-energy equivalence of 1% the mass of the sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so let’s not get greedy. I should also add that, \nwhile superluminal speeds are technically possible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868160,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for warp bubbles, in the words of the authors \nwould likely lead to pathologies—aka physics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875920,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breaking—and anyway require computational \nresources beyond this study to investigate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s some other good news: if we do detect \nthe gravitational wave signal of a bursting warp  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble, we may see more than just those waves. \nAfter the bubble collapses, the exotic matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "escapes—waves of positive and negative energy, \nwhich might lead to an electromagnetic counterpart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902560,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the gravitational wave signal. We call such \nsignals multimessenger events, and we routinely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "search for the other messengers—typically photons \nand neutrinos—for every gravitational wave we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915800,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect. We routinely do see the electromagnetic \ncounterpart of neutron star mergers for example."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923640,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if we never detect an alien warp \nsignal—perhaps because they don’t exist—this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928480,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "type of research is still interesting. Warp drives \nmay have their issues, but they are motivated by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the extremely well-tested theory of general \nrelativity. It’s instructive to poke at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938760,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "edges of such theories to test the limits \nof their validity and to come to a better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding of what’s actually possible, \nand what’s not. We can add new intuition and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949760,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "techniques to our toolbox for understanding \nthe possible universe. Or—far less likely,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956120,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but who knows—we build a high-frequency \ngravitational wave detector and immediately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960320,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "witness a gravitational pop, pop, pop—the \nunmistakable bursting of bubbles warped spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968800,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. We have a few new items for sale \nat the merch store. First up is a new limited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "edition wormhole enamel pin. If you'd like to \nshow the world that you'd rather instantly be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979440,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at some other patch of space time then you should \nsnap up this is a 1.5 inch enamel pin on 2x3 inch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holo foil backing card. Next up is our Quantum \nMechanics Officially Observer design, which will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992280,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let everyone know that you are in fact an official \nobserver of quantum mechanics. The design will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997840,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be featured on a t-shirt, a hoodie as well as our \nfirst ever 2.75 inch embroidered patch with velcro  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004200,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "backing. The t-shirt and hoodie will remain on the \nmerch store, but the wormhole pin and embroidered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009520,
    "dDurationMs": 9320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patch will be a limited edition pre-sale that \nwill ship in the first week of September."
    } ]
  } ]
}
